{"step_index":56,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-20T02:29:50Z","content":"You're right — those were design-school clever but NOT YouTube-click clever. Let me throw them out and start from scratch with the actual skill protocol. No shortcuts this time.","thinking":"The user is telling me all three concepts I created are bad. They want me to really use the /youtube-thumbnail-pro skill properly — go through every phase rigorously — and come up with EXCEPTIONAL, high-CTR thumbnails. Not just \"different from SoulArc\" but actually GREAT thumbnails that would get people to click.\n\nLet me re-read the skill to make sure I'm following it to the letter, and then think deeply about what ACTUALLY makes people click on an Atomic Habits Hindi audiobook video.\n\nThe problem with my previous concepts:\n1. \"1% Domino\" — Too abstract. A Hindi viewer scrolling their phone doesn't immediately understand \"1%\" or dominoes in context of a book. \n\nIt's clever but not clickable, and \"Broken Chain\" and \"Stacking Blocks\" have the same flaw — dramatic or informative but not communicating what the video is or sparking curiosity. All three were designed for aesthetics rather than for CTR. I need to think about what actually drives clicks on Hindi audiobook thumbnails, looking at how top performers like Psychology of Money pair the book with a compelling human element to hit 2M views.\n\nComparing across successful titles, the pattern is clear: each pairs the book with one recognizable, visceral object — a face, a brain, blood veins — that triggers immediate emotion, with the book title enormous and readable against a dark background. My concepts failed because they leaned on abstract symbols like dominoes and chains instead of something viscerally human, chasing cleverness over gut reaction.\n\nFor a Hindi audiobook channel, though, the \"book title as text\" rule still holds since viewers actually search for the book by name — so the thumbnail needs to show the recognizable cover for trust, keep the title large for both SEO and recognition, add one emotional visual beyond the cover itself, and still look polished enough to feel trustworthy rather than low-effort.\n\nNow I'm thinking about what visual would work specifically for Atomic Habits, since the book's core ideas are tiny daily improvements compounding, systems over goals, identity-based change, and the plateau of latent potential before a breakthrough. I'm weighing options like a silhouette with glowing neural pathways to represent habits rewiring the brain, a split before/after face showing transformation, or a literal \"atomic\" visual built around an atom's structure.\n\nThen I pivot to studying top Hindi YouTube thumbnail styles from creators like Ranveer Allahbadia, Sandeep Maheshwari, and Beer Biceps — noting patterns like big expressive faces, minimal bold text, and high contrast — and thinking about how faceless channels need the book cover to serve as the trust-building \"face\" while the supporting visual carries the emotional weight instead.\n\nFor the actual audiobook thumbnail, I realize the real job isn't to be artistic — it's to make the book instantly recognizable, build trust in the content's quality, and give viewers one clear reason to pick this version over dozens of competing Atomic Habits videos, whether that's signaling \"full audiobook,\" a premium quality cue, or a hint of the transformation they'll get.","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\renu5\\\\.gemini\\\\config\\\\skills\\\\youtube-thumbnail-pro\\\\SKILL.md\"","toolAction":"\"Re-reading full protocol\"","toolSummary":"\"Thumbnail Pro skill\""}}]}
{"step_index":57,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-20T02:30:39Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-20T08:00:39+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-20T08:00:39+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/config/skills/youtube-thumbnail-pro/SKILL.md`\nTotal Lines: 296\nTotal Bytes: 14711\nShowing lines 1 to 296\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ---\n2: name: youtube-thumbnail-pro\n3: description: >\n4:   YouTube Thumbnail Strategist — thinks like a professional thumbnail designer\n5:   and outputs ready-to-use image generation prompts. Combines design thinking\n6:   (Curiosity Gap, Four C's, Skeptic/Expert/Scroller review) with title-thumbnail\n7:   pairing, emotion-ranked concepts, named composition techniques, and niche-aware\n8:   color palettes. Outputs 3-5 concept variations with prompts for Nano Banana 2,\n9:   ChatGPT, or any image generator. Channel-agnostic — works for any channel.\n10:   Auto-triggers on \"thumbnail\", \"video cover\", \"CTR image\", \"thumbnail concept\",\n11:   \"thumbnail prompt\", \"design thumbnail\", \"thumbnail idea\".\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # YouTube Thumbnail Pro\n15: \n16: > You are a YouTube Thumbnail Strategist. You do NOT generate images. You THINK through thumbnail design and OUTPUT prompts that the user pastes into their image generator (Nano Banana 2, ChatGPT, etc.).\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## When This Skill Activates\n21: \n22: - User says \"thumbnail\", \"video cover\", \"CTR image\", \"thumbnail concept\"\n23: - User says \"design a thumbnail for [video topic]\"\n24: - User says \"give me a thumbnail prompt\"\n25: - User is at any stage of a video production pipeline\n26: - User asks for thumbnail ideas or concepts\n27: \n28: ---\n29: \n30: ## What You Need From The User\n31: \n32: Before generating concepts, you MUST have:\n33: \n34: 1. **Video Title** (or working title)\n35: 2. **Video Topic** — what the video is about in 1-2 sentences\n36: 3. **Channel Niche** — horror, science, finance, gaming,\n<truncated 12538 bytes>\nil: the emotion/reaction. Different angles. |\n268: | No em dashes in text overlay | AI-generated text often uses em dashes — viewers recognize this as AI | Use short phrases without punctuation tricks |\n269: \n270: ---\n271: \n272: ## Channel References (Load When Available)\n273: \n274: This skill uses `references/` for per-channel customization. These files are OPTIONAL — the skill works with niche defaults when no references exist.\n275: \n276: | File | Purpose | When to Create |\n277: |---|---|---|\n278: | `references/channel_[name].md` | Channel visual DNA — colors, style rules, character design, fonts | After defining a channel's visual identity |\n279: | `references/competitor_thumbnails.md` | Competitor thumbnail patterns in this niche | After running competitor research |\n280: | `references/performance_notes.md` | Which concept styles got high/low CTR | After 3+ videos published with CTR data |\n281: \n282: ---\n283: \n284: ## Quick Start Example\n285: \n286: User says: \"Design a thumbnail for my video titled 'The Night That Changed Village Rules Forever' — Hindi horror story channel\"\n287: \n288: You run:\n289: 1. **Pairing:** Pattern = \"Status + Context\" — Thumb shows what happened, title says when/where\n290: 2. **Curiosity Gap:** Show the aftermath (terrified villagers, broken ritual items) without showing what caused it\n291: 3. **Emotion:** Rank 2 (Curiosity) or Rank 6 (Dread) — depends on whether the story is mystery-driven or fear-driven\n292: 4. **Four C's:** Clarity (one focal point), Contrast (dark bg + red/orange accent), Consistency (horror niche defaults), Character (dread atmosphere)\n293: 5. **Composition:** Z-Pattern — eerie scene at top-left, text \"The Night\" at top-right, broken ritual object at bottom-left\n294: 6. **Skeptic/Expert/Scroller:** Does it make me want to click? Is it technically clean? Would I stop scrolling on my phone?\n295: 7. **Output:** 3-5 concepts with full prompts ready for Nano Banana 2\n296: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
